      "message": "High level summary\n\nThis patch removes the Dolby “fix” helper script and all of its plumbing:\n\t•\tDeletes rootdir/bin/init.dolby_fix.sh\n\t•\tRemoves the corresponding service and start call from init.qcom.rc\n\t•\tRemoves the SELinux file context and domain policy for the script\n\t•\tStops copying the script into the vendor image from sm6150.mk\n\nEffectively, /data/vendor/dolby will no longer be wiped on upgrade by that script. The change reduces attack surface and complexity, but may reintroduce the original functional issue that motivated the script.\n\nBelow is a breakdown by area.\n\n⸻\n\n1. Functional behavior\n\nWhat changes\n\nPreviously:\n\t•\tOn post-fs-data, init ran vendor.init-dolby_fix-sh once per boot.\n\t•\tThat script did:\n\t•\trm -rf /data/vendor/dolby/* if persist.vendor.audio.dolby_fix_applied !\u003d \"true\"\n\t•\tSet persist.vendor.audio.dolby_fix_applied\u003dtrue after the first run so it was effectively a one time data wipe.\n\nAfter this patch:\n\t•\tThe service vendor.init-dolby_fix-sh is gone, and init.dolby_fix.sh is not installed.\n\t•\t/data/vendor/dolby is no longer wiped or touched at boot.\n\nPotential issues\n\t1.\tReintroduction of the original Dolby bug\nThe commit being reverted was specifically about “Remove /data/vendor/dolby on upgrade”. That was likely added to fix a migration or corruption issue in Dolby data. Removing it can reintroduce:\n\t•\tOld or incompatible Dolby data under /data/vendor/dolby after an OTA.\n\t•\tPotential crashes or misbehavior in Dolby services if they do not robustly handle stale data.\nSuggestion:\n\t•\tDocument in the commit message why reverting this is safe now, for example:\n\t•\tThe upstream Dolby code has been fixed to handle old data.\n\t•\tThe device tree no longer uses Dolby.\n\t•\tIf the original problem still exists for some users, consider replacing the script with a more targeted, version based migration (for example, only wipe specific files or only when upgrading across a problematic version range).\n\t2.\tPersisted property left unused\nThe property persist.vendor.audio.dolby_fix_applied will remain set on devices that already ran the old script, but nothing references it anymore.\nThis is not harmful, but it is dead configuration.\nSuggestion:\n\t•\tOptionally add a short note in a future clean up change or in documentation that this property is obsolete, to prevent confusion during debugging.\n\n⸻\n\n2. Security considerations\n\nFrom a security perspective this patch is strictly an improvement:\n\t•\tThe removed script ran as a dedicated domain vendor_init-dolby-sh with:\n\t•\tallow ... vendor_data_file:{dir,file} { rw_* perms setattr unlink }\n\t•\tself:capability { fowner fsetid }\n\t•\tdontaudit ... { dac_override dac_read_search }\n\t•\tIt executed rm -rf /data/vendor/dolby/* using /vendor/bin/sh and toolbox, which is broad in effect.\n\nPotential risks in the old code:\n\t•\tIf /data/vendor/dolby could be replaced with or contain symlinks, a bug or misconfiguration could let the script remove unintended files.\n\t•\tThe broad SELinux permissions on the generic vendor_data_file type made the domain more powerful than necessary.\n\nBy removing the script and its domain:\n\t•\tYou eliminate those extra capabilities and SELinux rules.\n\t•\tYou reduce early boot attack surface.\n\nNo new security vulnerabilities are introduced.\n\n⸻\n\n3. SELinux and init integration\n\nChanges\n\t•\tfile_contexts:\n\t•\tRemoves:\n\n# Dolby Fix script\n/vendor/bin/init\\.dolby_fix\\.sh u:object_r:vendor_init-dolby-sh_exec:s0\n\n\n\t•\tKeeps the /data/vendor/dolby label, which is correct and needed.\n\n\t•\tinit-qcom-dolby-sh.te:\n\t•\tEntirely removed, including:\n\t•\ttype vendor_init-dolby-sh, domain;\n\t•\ttype vendor_init-dolby-sh_exec, ...;\n\t•\tinit_daemon_domain(vendor_init-dolby-sh)\n\t•\tAll allow and set_prop rules.\n\t•\tinit.qcom.rc:\n\t•\tRemoves the start vendor.init-dolby_fix-sh in on post-fs-data.\n\t•\tRemoves the service vendor.init-dolby_fix-sh block.\n\nPotential issues\n\t1.\tDangling references to SELinux types\nIf there are any other policy files still referring to vendor_init-dolby-sh or vendor_init-dolby-sh_exec, the policy build will fail.\nSuggestion:\n\t•\tRun a quick grep -R \"vendor_init-dolby-sh\" sepolicy across the tree to confirm there are no remaining references.\n\t2.\tBuild system references to the .te file\nSome projects enumerate TE files explicitly rather than via globs.\nSuggestion:\n\t•\tCheck any Android.bp or sepolicy.mk related to vendor sepolicy to ensure init-qcom-dolby-sh.te is not listed explicitly. If it is, remove it there as well.\n\nAssuming those are clean, the SELinux and init changes are correct and consistent.\n\n⸻\n\n4. Build and packaging\n\nIn sm6150.mk:\n\t•\tDolby section removes:\n\n# Dolby\nPRODUCT_COPY_FILES +\u003d \\\n    $(LOCAL_PATH)/rootdir/bin/init.dolby_fix.sh:$(TARGET_COPY_OUT_VENDOR)/bin/init.dolby_fix.sh\n\n\n\nSince the file itself is deleted, removing it from PRODUCT_COPY_FILES is necessary and correct. There are no other obvious references to init.dolby_fix.sh in the patch, so packaging is consistent.\n\n⸻\n\n5. Style and commit message\n\nThe code being removed previously followed the existing style of the device tree:\n\t•\tShell script header and SPDX line are standard.\n\t•\tInit rc block and sepolicy use conventional formatting.\n\nFor this patch itself:\n\t•\tSubject and body are syntactically valid for a revert. However, it only contains the auto generated line:\n\nThis reverts commit 06517e7f66d786ca2ba7ef74c688aabc62f4554b.\n\n\n\t•\tThere is no explanation of why the revert is needed.\n\nSuggestion:\n\t•\tAdd a short rationale to help future maintainers, such as:\n\t•\tDolby data no longer needs to be wiped because of upstream fixes\n\t•\tThe fix caused user configuration loss\n\t•\tThe script was causing boot time regressions or SELinux issues\n\nExample:\n\nThis reverts commit 06517e7f66d7 (\"sm6150-common: rootdir: Remove /data/vendor/dolby on upgrade\").\n\nThe one time wipe of /data/vendor/dolby is no longer necessary and has been\ncausing loss of user Dolby settings after OTA updates. Dolby now handles\nstale data internally, so we can safely drop the helper script and its\nSELinux domain.\n\nThe Change-Id line is fine if this is going through Gerrit.\n\n⸻\n\n6. Testing recommendations\n\nBecause this is an early boot and audio related change, I would recommend at least:\n\t1.\tClean flash test\n\t•\tFlash the build from this change on a device with an empty /data.\n\t•\tVerify system boots cleanly.\n\t•\tConfirm Dolby functionality (if Dolby is enabled on this device tree):\n\t•\tAudio playback\n\t•\tDolby toggles and profiles\n\t•\tNetflix or other DRM protected apps if relevant.\n\t2.\tOTA upgrade scenario\n\t•\tUpgrade from a build that has the Dolby fix script to a build with this revert.\n\t•\tConfirm:\n\t•\tNo boot loops or SELinux denials due to missing script or domain.\n\t•\tDolby still functions correctly and user settings are preserved.\n\t•\tAlso test upgrading from a build that never had the script (for users skipping releases).\n\t3.\tSELinux and log checks\n\t•\tCheck dmesg and logcat for:\n\t•\tavc: denied entries mentioning Dolby or /data/vendor/dolby.\n\t•\tAny init errors about missing services or binaries related to Dolby.\n\n⸻\n\nOverall assessment\n\t•\tThe patch is internally consistent and correctly removes the Dolby fix script and its plumbing.\n\t•\tIt reduces security risk and complexity by removing a privileged early boot helper.\n\t•\tThe main potential downside is functional regression if the original reason for wiping /data/vendor/dolby still applies.\n\nAs long as you are confident the underlying Dolby issue has been resolved or is no longer relevant, this revert looks technically sound. I would only recommend adding a short explanation to the commit message and double checking there are no stray references to vendor_init-dolby-sh in sepolicy or build files.",
